# Gaudium @VERSION@: install notes

This archive is Gaudium @VERSION@ for the Intel Gaudi 2 (HL-225), built for Linux x86_64 and
tested with release @SDK_RELEASE@ of the Intel Gaudi SDK.

Gaudium is in the middle of a port to Rust (see `README.md`): interfaces and flags still
change, and this is not a stable release.

## What is in the archive

| Path | What |
|---|---|
| `bin/gaudium` | The engine and its HTTP server (one binary). |
| `kernels/` | The **sources** of Gaudium's TPC kernels (`tpc/`) and graph-compiler glue libraries (`glue/`), with their `Makefile`. You compile them (below). |
| `ui/` | The Web UI the server serves. |
| `docs/` | The command-line and environment reference, and the notes on the MCP proxy and HTTPS. |
| `BUILD-INFO.txt` | The commit and the tool versions of this build. |
| `SHA256SUMS` | The SHA-256 of every file above (`sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS`). |
| `LICENSE`, `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` | Gaudium's licence (MIT) and the notices of the third-party code that is included. |

## What is not in it

- **The Intel Gaudi software.** Gaudium runs on top of Intel's driver, firmware and SDK
  (SynapseAI). Their licence does not allow redistribution: you install them yourself, from
  Intel.
- **Compiled kernels.** The kernels and glue libraries are compiled with the SDK's own
  compiler and header, so the archive has their sources and you build them once with your SDK
  installation.

## Requirements

- A host with an Intel Gaudi 2 card.
- The Intel Gaudi software, release **@SDK_RELEASE@** (package version `@SDK_VERSION@`) being the
  one this build was tested with: `habanalabs-dkms` (driver), `habanalabs-firmware`,
  `habanalabs-graph` (SynapseAI, the graph compiler and the headers under
  `/usr/include/habanalabs`) and `habanalabs-tools` (`tpc-clang`). Follow the
  [Intel Gaudi installation guide](https://docs.habana.ai/en/latest/Installation_Guide/index.html);
  the packages come from [Intel's package repository](https://vault.habana.ai/).
- `make` and `g++` to build the kernels.
- Linux x86_64 with glibc @GLIBC_MIN@ or later (this build was made on @OS_NAME@).
- A model checkpoint directory (see "Status" in `README.md` for the supported models).

## Install

```bash
sha256sum -c @NAME@.tar.gz.sha256        # next to the downloaded archive
tar -xzf @NAME@.tar.gz
cd @NAME@

# Kernels and glue libraries, compiled with your SDK installation into build/kernels/
make -C kernels -j"$(nproc)"
```

Build the kernels again after installing another SDK release.

## Run

```bash
bin/gaudium --version
bin/gaudium --help                       # every flag and environment variable
bin/gaudium                              # verified-core self-check (no card needed)
bin/gaudium --hpu-smoke                  # small live test on the card

bin/gaudium --serve --model <model_dir> --kernels "$PWD/build/kernels" --ui-dir "$PWD/ui" \
            --cache-dir <cache_dir> --max-ctx 32768    # API + Web UI on port 8090
```

`--cache-dir` keeps the compiled recipes between starts; without it every start compiles them
again. Use a new cache directory after installing another SDK release.

## HTTPS

Opened from another machine, the Web UI needs HTTPS for the microphone and the clipboard. The
server serves HTTPS on the same port once it has a certificate, and `--make-cert` makes one
(it needs the system's OpenSSL 3, `libssl3`):

```bash
bin/gaudium --make-cert <dir> --cert-names <host name>,<address>    # once
bin/gaudium --serve ... --tls-cert <dir>/server.crt --tls-key <dir>/server.key
```

`--make-cert` prints the certificate's fingerprints and how to trust `<dir>/ca.crt` on each
kind of device (once per device). For a new name or address later, run it again with
`--cert-overwrite`: the devices need nothing new. `docs/mcp-proxy-https.md` has the details.

The engine loads `libSynapse.so` from `/usr/lib/habanalabs/` or the loader's search path
(set `GAUDIUM_SYNAPSE_LIB` to use another file). Only one process may open the card at a time.

The archive can be unpacked anywhere. `docs/reference.md` is the full `--help` text.
